WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:12.113
[relaxing music]

00:01:15.158 --> 00:01:17.952
Regardless of where you like
or in what time period you look,

00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:19.996
you'll find that
humans are always making music,

00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:23.166
and the music that we make
is similar in certain ways too.

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:25.210 line:76%
And this sort of thing
might seem obvious to us

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.504 line:76%
just because it's
what we're used to already.

00:01:27.504 --> 00:01:29.464 line:76%
But when we see this
kind of global pattern,

00:01:29.464 --> 00:01:31.716 line:76%
it suggests that there's
something in our biology

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:33.510 line:76%
and in how
we humans have evolved

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.470
that makes it
so commonplace in the world.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.556
So if some song types
like lullabies are grounded

00:01:38.556 --> 00:01:40.391
in human biology and evolution,

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:42.101
does that mean that they can
then transcend

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:44.062
cultural
and linguistic barriers?

00:01:44.062 --> 00:01:45.146
And if that's the case,

00:01:45.146 --> 00:01:48.149
are these types of human songs
a universal language?

00:01:48.191 --> 00:01:51.069
So to test this question,
what we did is we put together

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:54.948
a large archive of 118
recordings of songs

00:01:55.031 --> 00:01:56.199
recorded all around the world.

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:56.699
They were used

00:01:56.699 --> 00:01:58.117
as dance songs, lullabies,

00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:01.120
healing songs and love songs
in their original locations.

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.998
And we recorded these songs
from 86 different societies

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:04.833
around the globe.

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:07.460
So a really diverse
set of musical stimuli.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.003
And then we took these songs
and played them

00:02:09.003 --> 00:02:11.381
to an equally diverse
group of participants.

00:02:11.381 --> 00:02:15.677
So we have around 6,000 people
from 49 industrialized societies

00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:18.680
and three relatively
isolated societies.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.015
And so in total,
we have about 31 languages

00:02:21.015 --> 00:02:22.016
that these people are speaking.

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:24.936
So this is a really diverse,
representative group of people.

00:02:24.936 --> 00:02:26.396
And we asked
all of these participants

00:02:26.396 --> 00:02:28.690
to listen
to each of the songs and choose

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.568
what context they thought the
song was originally used in.

00:02:31.568 --> 00:02:33.820
And what we found
when we analyzed the data

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.905
that we had
was that regardless of where

00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:37.448
in the world
these participants were from

00:02:37.448 --> 00:02:38.950
or what language they spoke,

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.452
participants were able
to identify dance songs,

00:02:41.452 --> 00:02:44.455
lullabies and healing songs
all at above chance levels.

00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:46.958
We in our lab
think that it's because music

00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:49.294
probably evolved around
two main functions.

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:52.714
One would be in the context
of soothing babies,

00:02:52.714 --> 00:02:55.425
because song is such an honest
signal of you

00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:57.302
actually attending to them
and showing you that.

00:02:57.302 --> 00:02:58.970
Showing them
that you care. Right.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.138
So lullabies

00:03:00.138 --> 00:03:01.055
may have developed

00:03:01.055 --> 00:03:03.016
as a really honest
signal of affection

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:04.225
and being that
for your children.

00:03:04.225 --> 00:03:06.811
So we think that's one way
that music may have developed.

00:03:06.811 --> 00:03:10.315
But we also think that song
may have developed in tandem

00:03:10.315 --> 00:03:13.318
with dance and in creating
coalition and human groups.

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:15.194
So this would have
a really strong

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:16.362
evolutionary pressure, right?

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:19.240
If you want human groups to be
well coordinated.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.742
You want to be able
to coordinate

00:03:20.742 --> 00:03:21.951
bodily motion together.

00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:25.330
You want to encourage
things like shared rhythm,

00:03:25.413 --> 00:03:26.831
synchronizing work.

00:03:26.831 --> 00:03:29.334
And music just functions
as a very efficient way

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:31.753
to get people in sync
in that sense.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:34.005
I think the main takeaway
is that we have evidence

00:03:34.005 --> 00:03:36.215
that human song
is this really fundamental

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:38.968
aspect of the human experience
and is likely shaped

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:40.720
by our biology
and human evolution.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.306
So this is really something
so fundamental to humanity

00:03:43.306 --> 00:03:46.351
that also seems to unite us
in the way that we go about it.

